       glibc are not described) and likely out of date.

       The functions described below make queries to  and  interpret  the  re‐
       sponses from Internet domain name servers.

       The  API  consists  of a set of more modern, reentrant functions and an
       older set of nonreentrant functions that  have  been  superseded.   The
       traditional  resolver interfaces such as rreess__iinniitt() and rreess__qquueerryy() use
       some static (global) state stored  in  the  ___r_e_s  structure,  rendering
       these  functions non‐thread‐safe.  BIND 8.2 introduced a set of new in‐
       terfaces rreess__nniinniitt(), rreess__nnqquueerryy(), and so on, which take  a  _r_e_s___s_t_a_t_e
       as their first argument, so you can use a per‐thread resolver state.

       The  rreess__nniinniitt()  and rreess__iinniitt() functions read the configuration files
       (see rreessoollvv..ccoonnff(5)) to get the default domain name and name server ad‐
       dress(es).  If no server is given, the local host is tried.  If no  do‐
       main  is given, that associated with the local host is used.  It can be
       overridden with the environment variable LLOOCCAALLDDOOMMAAIINN.   rreess__nniinniitt()  or
       rreess__iinniitt()  is  normally executed by the first call to one of the other
       functions.  Every call to rreess__nniinniitt() requires a corresponding call  to
       rreess__nncclloossee()  to  free  memory  allocated by rreess__nniinniitt() and subsequent
       calls to rreess__nnqquueerryy().

       The rreess__nnqquueerryy() and rreess__qquueerryy() functions query the  name  server  for
       the  fully qualified domain name _n_a_m_e of specified _t_y_p_e and _c_l_a_s_s.  The
       reply is left in the buffer _a_n_s_w_e_r of length  _a_n_s_l_e_n  supplied  by  the
       caller.

       The rreess__nnsseeaarrcchh() and rreess__sseeaarrcchh() functions make a query and waits for
       the  response  like  rreess__nnqquueerryy() and rreess__qquueerryy(), but in addition they
       implement the default and search rules controlled by  RREESS__DDEEFFNNAAMMEESS  and
       RREESS__DDNNSSRRCCHH (see description of ___r_e_s options below).

       The rreess__nnqquueerryyddoommaaiinn() and rreess__qquueerryyddoommaaiinn() functions make a query us‐
